1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,508
How does a small update for an

2
00:00:02,508 --> 00:00:05,206
accounting program in Ukraine end up

3
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:07,364
stopping trucks at a port in New

4
00:00:07,364 --> 00:00:08,146
Jersey,

5
00:00:08,146 --> 00:00:10,844
shutting down drug production at one of

6
00:00:10,844 --> 00:00:12,975
the world's biggest drug makers,

7
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:15,133
and costing the world more than ten

8
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:16,812
billion dollars?

9
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:24,521
The code was called NotPetya,

10
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:26,743
and the White House later put its

11
00:00:26,743 --> 00:00:30,120
damage at more than ten billion dollars.

12
00:00:30,210 --> 00:00:31,768
One victim was Maersk,

13
00:00:31,768 --> 00:00:35,010
the world's largest shipping conglomerate.

14
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:36,521
Within half an hour,

15
00:00:36,521 --> 00:00:38,721
its staff were running down the hallways,

16
00:00:38,721 --> 00:00:40,943
shouting at colleagues to unplug their

17
00:00:40,943 --> 00:00:42,210
computers.

18
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:44,364
And what saved it in the end

19
00:00:44,364 --> 00:00:46,720
was a single server in Africa that

20
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,819
happened to be offline.

21
00:00:54,483 --> 00:00:57,326
How do you hit an entire country

22
00:00:57,326 --> 00:00:58,558
at once?

23
00:01:03,483 --> 00:01:07,153
You go through software everybody trusts.

24
00:01:07,203 --> 00:01:09,918
A small family-run company in Kyiv,

25
00:01:09,918 --> 00:01:10,808
Linkos Group,

26
00:01:10,808 --> 00:01:13,523
made an accounting program called M.E.Doc,

27
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:16,717
used by Ukrainian businesses to file their

28
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:17,773
taxes.

29
00:01:17,863 --> 00:01:18,335
Like most software,

30
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:19,343
it updated itself.

31
00:01:19,343 --> 00:01:21,698
In the spring of twenty seventeen,

32
00:01:21,698 --> 00:01:24,766
according to American prosecutors,

33
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:27,620
Russian military hackers took over its

34
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:29,998
update servers and slipped their own code

35
00:01:29,998 --> 00:01:32,026
into the updates.

36
00:01:32,283 --> 00:01:33,891
On June twenty-seventh,

37
00:01:33,891 --> 00:01:36,688
the day before a national holiday in

38
00:01:36,688 --> 00:01:37,364
Ukraine,

39
00:01:37,364 --> 00:01:39,363
they used that back door.

40
00:01:39,593 --> 00:01:42,783
The malware spread with terrifying speed.

41
00:01:42,983 --> 00:01:45,853
A Ukrainian security firm watched it bring

42
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:47,619
down the network of a large bank

43
00:01:47,619 --> 00:01:49,543
in forty-five seconds.

44
00:01:49,753 --> 00:01:52,019
Part of a major transit hub was

45
00:01:52,019 --> 00:01:55,136
fully infected in sixteen seconds.

46
00:01:55,383 --> 00:01:57,473
It had two weapons.

47
00:01:57,473 --> 00:01:58,981
The first was EternalBlue,

48
00:01:58,981 --> 00:02:01,384
a hacking tool built by America's own

49
00:02:01,384 --> 00:02:03,765
National Security Agency,

50
00:02:03,765 --> 00:02:06,113
which had leaked earlier that year.

51
00:02:06,123 --> 00:02:08,250
It broke into Windows computers that had

52
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:10,783
not installed a security fix.

53
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:11,632
The second,

54
00:02:11,632 --> 00:02:12,431
Mimikatz,

55
00:02:12,431 --> 00:02:14,459
pulled passwords out of memory,

56
00:02:14,459 --> 00:02:16,713
and those passwords opened the machines

57
00:02:16,713 --> 00:02:18,337
that had been fixed.

58
00:02:18,583 --> 00:02:21,384
Let me draw how one computer becomes

59
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:22,493
thousands.

60
00:02:22,583 --> 00:02:25,009
One infected machine finds a neighbor that

61
00:02:25,009 --> 00:02:26,971
missed the security fix,

62
00:02:26,971 --> 00:02:28,233
and breaks in.

63
00:02:28,313 --> 00:02:28,743
There,

64
00:02:28,743 --> 00:02:31,833
it reads the passwords stored in memory.

65
00:02:32,163 --> 00:02:34,536
Those passwords open a third machine,

66
00:02:34,536 --> 00:02:37,293
even a fully updated one.

67
00:02:37,403 --> 00:02:40,273
Every victim becomes a new attacker.

68
00:02:40,523 --> 00:02:42,513
And inside a big company,

69
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:45,173
where every computer trusts the others,

70
00:02:45,173 --> 00:02:47,338
there is no wall to stop it.

71
00:02:47,583 --> 00:02:50,593
Then the screens changed.

72
00:02:50,793 --> 00:02:53,455
Some said the computer was repairing its

73
00:02:53,455 --> 00:02:55,023
file system.

74
00:02:55,183 --> 00:02:56,108
Others said,

75
00:02:56,108 --> 00:02:56,559
oops,

76
00:02:56,559 --> 00:02:59,146
your important files are encrypted,

77
00:02:59,146 --> 00:03:01,518
and asked for three hundred dollars in

78
00:03:01,518 --> 00:03:02,593
bitcoin.

79
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:04,723
But it was a disguise.

80
00:03:04,783 --> 00:03:06,574
According to the charges,

81
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:08,615
the files were scrambled in a way

82
00:03:08,615 --> 00:03:11,483
that not even the hackers could undo.

83
00:03:11,783 --> 00:03:13,813
Paying changed nothing.

84
00:03:13,943 --> 00:03:15,763
This was not a ransom.

85
00:03:15,763 --> 00:03:17,343
It was a wiper.

86
00:03:17,583 --> 00:03:18,466
In Ukraine,

87
00:03:18,466 --> 00:03:19,803
it hit hospitals,

88
00:03:19,803 --> 00:03:21,366
six power companies,

89
00:03:21,366 --> 00:03:22,249
two airports,

90
00:03:22,249 --> 00:03:23,812
more than twenty-two banks,

91
00:03:23,812 --> 00:03:24,922
cash machines,

92
00:03:24,922 --> 00:03:28,693
and practically every government agency.

93
00:03:28,943 --> 00:03:30,825
It even shut down the computers at

94
00:03:30,825 --> 00:03:32,743
the Chernobyl clean-up site.

95
00:03:32,873 --> 00:03:35,338
One senior official estimated that ten

96
00:03:35,338 --> 00:03:37,393
percent of all the computers in the

97
00:03:37,393 --> 00:03:39,020
country were wiped.

98
00:03:39,283 --> 00:03:41,422
But the code did not stay in

99
00:03:41,422 --> 00:03:42,633
Ukraine.

100
00:03:42,673 --> 00:03:44,034
It got on a plane,

101
00:03:44,034 --> 00:03:44,934
so to speak,

102
00:03:44,934 --> 00:03:48,072
inside the networks of global companies.

103
00:03:53,717 --> 00:03:56,779
What happens when the company behind a

104
00:03:56,779 --> 00:03:59,007
fifth of world shipping loses its

105
00:03:59,007 --> 00:04:00,442
computers?

106
00:04:02,717 --> 00:04:06,717
Maersk's door was a single computer.

107
00:04:06,957 --> 00:04:09,940
A finance executive at its office in

108
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:12,427
Ukraine had asked for M.E.Doc to be

109
00:04:12,427 --> 00:04:14,767
installed on one machine.

110
00:04:15,157 --> 00:04:16,617
That was enough.

111
00:04:16,737 --> 00:04:18,448
Through Maersk's global network,

112
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:20,928
the malware reached its headquarters in

113
00:04:20,928 --> 00:04:24,376
Copenhagen on a sunny summer afternoon,

114
00:04:24,376 --> 00:04:25,343
and from there,

115
00:04:25,343 --> 00:04:27,679
the rest of the company.

116
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:30,061
This was the world's largest shipping

117
00:04:30,061 --> 00:04:31,374
conglomerate,

118
00:04:31,374 --> 00:04:34,028
with seventy-six port terminals,

119
00:04:34,028 --> 00:04:36,145
nearly eight hundred vessels,

120
00:04:36,145 --> 00:04:38,021
and close to a fifth of the

121
00:04:38,021 --> 00:04:40,497
world's shipping capacity.

122
00:04:40,767 --> 00:04:43,150
Every container is tracked,

123
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:45,917
booked and cleared by computers.

124
00:04:45,997 --> 00:04:48,581
And the computers were gone.

125
00:04:48,817 --> 00:04:51,505
At the terminal in Elizabeth,

126
00:04:51,505 --> 00:04:52,467
New Jersey,

127
00:04:52,467 --> 00:04:54,934
about three thousand trucks arrive on a

128
00:04:54,934 --> 00:04:55,987
good day.

129
00:04:55,987 --> 00:04:56,432
That day,

130
00:04:56,432 --> 00:04:58,107
the gates stopped working.

131
00:04:58,137 --> 00:05:00,518
Hundreds of trucks backed up for miles,

132
00:05:00,518 --> 00:05:02,703
and the terminal was closed for the

133
00:05:02,703 --> 00:05:04,177
rest of the day.

134
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:06,971
The same thing happened at seventeen of

135
00:05:06,971 --> 00:05:09,103
Maersk's seventy-six terminals,

136
00:05:09,103 --> 00:05:12,577
from Los Angeles to Rotterdam and Mumbai.

137
00:05:12,707 --> 00:05:14,047
Gates down.

138
00:05:14,047 --> 00:05:15,870
Cranes frozen.

139
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:19,057
The ships themselves kept sailing.

140
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:21,188
But without the systems that say which

141
00:05:21,188 --> 00:05:22,106
box goes where,

142
00:05:22,106 --> 00:05:24,807
a port becomes a parking lot.

143
00:05:24,807 --> 00:05:26,993
Staff fell back on personal email,

144
00:05:26,993 --> 00:05:28,297
spreadsheets and WhatsApp.

145
00:05:28,297 --> 00:05:31,047
One customer remembered booking five

146
00:05:31,047 --> 00:05:33,297
hundred shipping containers through a

147
00:05:33,297 --> 00:05:34,897
messaging app.

148
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:36,172
To come back,

149
00:05:36,172 --> 00:05:39,100
Maersk had to rebuild everything.

150
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:40,127
And for that,

151
00:05:40,127 --> 00:05:42,233
it needed one thing it could not

152
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:43,096
find.

153
00:05:48,750 --> 00:05:52,577
What do you rebuild first when everything

154
00:05:52,577 --> 00:05:53,873
is gone?

155
00:05:57,750 --> 00:05:59,674
Maersk turned an office building in

156
00:05:59,674 --> 00:06:00,507
Maidenhead,

157
00:06:00,507 --> 00:06:01,982
a town west of London,

158
00:06:01,982 --> 00:06:04,547
into a recovery center run with the

159
00:06:04,547 --> 00:06:06,430
consultancy Deloitte.

160
00:06:06,610 --> 00:06:09,113
Staff kept arriving from all over the

161
00:06:09,113 --> 00:06:09,589
world,

162
00:06:09,589 --> 00:06:11,190
luggage in hand.

163
00:06:11,270 --> 00:06:12,791
The good news was that there were

164
00:06:12,791 --> 00:06:15,181
backups of almost all of the company's

165
00:06:15,181 --> 00:06:16,216
servers.

166
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:18,960
The bad news was the map.

167
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:21,597
A network like Maersk's runs on special

168
00:06:21,597 --> 00:06:24,322
servers called domain controllers,

169
00:06:24,322 --> 00:06:26,524
the master list of who may log

170
00:06:26,524 --> 00:06:27,950
in to what.

171
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:30,734
Maersk had about a hundred and fifty,

172
00:06:30,734 --> 00:06:32,994
copying each other so that any one

173
00:06:32,994 --> 00:06:34,950
could replace the others.

174
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,373
That design covered every disaster except

175
00:06:38,373 --> 00:06:39,200
one.

176
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,920
All of them wiped at once.

177
00:06:41,930 --> 00:06:44,783
And there was no separate backup.

178
00:06:45,017 --> 00:06:47,227
Let me draw the trap.

179
00:06:47,227 --> 00:06:48,580
Here are the servers,

180
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:49,703
each one a box,

181
00:06:49,703 --> 00:06:52,367
all connected to the master list.

182
00:06:52,417 --> 00:06:53,255
Normally,

183
00:06:53,255 --> 00:06:54,523
if one copy dies,

184
00:06:54,523 --> 00:06:56,197
the others fill in.

185
00:06:56,317 --> 00:06:58,645
But the malware reached every copy at

186
00:06:58,645 --> 00:06:59,767
the same time.

187
00:06:59,767 --> 00:07:01,937
No healthy copy was left.

188
00:07:01,947 --> 00:07:03,636
And without the master list,

189
00:07:03,636 --> 00:07:05,774
the backups of all the other servers

190
00:07:05,774 --> 00:07:06,822
were useless,

191
00:07:06,822 --> 00:07:09,173
because nobody could log in to restore

192
00:07:09,173 --> 00:07:09,987
them.

193
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:13,048
After calling hundreds of administrators

194
00:07:13,048 --> 00:07:14,191
around the world,

195
00:07:14,191 --> 00:07:16,190
they found one survivor.

196
00:07:16,250 --> 00:07:18,746
A single domain controller in an office

197
00:07:18,746 --> 00:07:19,970
in Ghana.

198
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:22,311
A power cut had knocked it offline,

199
00:07:22,311 --> 00:07:24,720
and the malware never touched it.

200
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:27,238
A blackout had saved the company.

201
00:07:27,483 --> 00:07:30,028
But the connection in Ghana was so

202
00:07:30,028 --> 00:07:32,319
slow that sending the data would take

203
00:07:32,319 --> 00:07:33,153
days.

204
00:07:33,153 --> 00:07:35,133
So they decided to fly it.

205
00:07:35,193 --> 00:07:37,324
Nobody in the Ghana office had a

206
00:07:37,324 --> 00:07:38,273
British visa,

207
00:07:38,273 --> 00:07:40,423
so it became a relay race.

208
00:07:40,483 --> 00:07:42,193
One employee flew the hard drive to

209
00:07:42,193 --> 00:07:44,545
Nigeria and handed it over at the

210
00:07:44,545 --> 00:07:46,020
airport to a colleague,

211
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:47,731
who carried it on a six and

212
00:07:47,731 --> 00:07:49,827
a half hours flight to London.

213
00:07:50,083 --> 00:07:51,128
With that drive,

214
00:07:51,128 --> 00:07:53,271
Maidenhead could start bringing the

215
00:07:53,271 --> 00:07:54,943
company back.

216
00:07:55,083 --> 00:07:57,473
According to Maersk's chairman,

217
00:07:57,473 --> 00:07:59,894
it took ten days to rebuild a

218
00:07:59,894 --> 00:08:02,617
network of four thousand servers and

219
00:08:02,617 --> 00:08:05,033
forty-five thousand PCs.

220
00:08:05,213 --> 00:08:08,818
Full recovery took close to two months.

221
00:08:09,083 --> 00:08:10,863
Then came the bill.

222
00:08:10,863 --> 00:08:12,741
And Maersk was only one of the

223
00:08:12,741 --> 00:08:13,810
victims.

224
00:08:19,450 --> 00:08:22,182
How much can a few lines of

225
00:08:22,182 --> 00:08:23,465
code cost?

226
00:08:28,450 --> 00:08:29,478
In August,

227
00:08:29,478 --> 00:08:32,115
Maersk told investors the attack would cut

228
00:08:32,115 --> 00:08:34,488
its results by two hundred to three

229
00:08:34,488 --> 00:08:36,670
hundred million dollars.

230
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:39,395
Its chairman later put it between two

231
00:08:39,395 --> 00:08:41,406
hundred and fifty and three hundred

232
00:08:41,406 --> 00:08:42,900
million dollars.

233
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:46,315
Some of the company's own staff privately

234
00:08:46,315 --> 00:08:49,192
suspected even that was too low.

235
00:08:49,450 --> 00:08:51,820
And the list kept growing.

236
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:53,133
The drug maker Merck,

237
00:08:53,133 --> 00:08:55,469
whose ability to manufacture some

238
00:08:55,469 --> 00:08:58,209
medicines was temporarily shut down,

239
00:08:58,209 --> 00:09:00,333
reported a loss of eight hundred and

240
00:09:00,333 --> 00:09:02,420
seventy million dollars.

241
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:03,229
FedEx,

242
00:09:03,229 --> 00:09:05,854
through its European arm TNT Express,

243
00:09:05,854 --> 00:09:08,310
four hundred million dollars.

244
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:09,261
Saint-Gobain,

245
00:09:09,261 --> 00:09:10,653
the French building giant,

246
00:09:10,653 --> 00:09:12,778
three hundred and eighty-four million

247
00:09:12,778 --> 00:09:13,830
dollars.

248
00:09:13,830 --> 00:09:15,574
The snack maker Mondelez,

249
00:09:15,574 --> 00:09:17,340
one hundred and eighty-eight million

250
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:18,382
dollars.

251
00:09:18,617 --> 00:09:20,697
Let me draw the scale.

252
00:09:21,027 --> 00:09:23,321
Here is a typical ransomware attack that

253
00:09:23,321 --> 00:09:25,596
paralyzed the city of Atlanta,

254
00:09:25,596 --> 00:09:27,917
costing up to ten million dollars.

255
00:09:27,987 --> 00:09:29,149
Next to it,

256
00:09:29,149 --> 00:09:29,917
Maersk.

257
00:09:29,917 --> 00:09:31,177
Then FedEx.

258
00:09:31,177 --> 00:09:32,447
Then Merck.

259
00:09:32,447 --> 00:09:33,947
And then the total.

260
00:09:33,947 --> 00:09:35,850
The White House estimate of more than

261
00:09:35,850 --> 00:09:37,507
ten billion dollars.

262
00:09:37,667 --> 00:09:38,751
Next to that bar,

263
00:09:38,751 --> 00:09:42,007
the Atlanta attack is barely a line.

264
00:09:42,067 --> 00:09:43,613
It is about a tenth of one

265
00:09:43,613 --> 00:09:44,600
percent.

266
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:47,067
Not all the damage was measured in

267
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:48,110
money.

268
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:49,517
In Pennsylvania,

269
00:09:49,517 --> 00:09:51,953
NotPetya hit the Heritage Valley Health

270
00:09:51,953 --> 00:09:52,595
System,

271
00:09:52,595 --> 00:09:54,810
two hospitals and seventy-eight other

272
00:09:54,810 --> 00:09:56,830
medical facilities.

273
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:58,116
For more than a week,

274
00:09:58,116 --> 00:10:00,297
staff could not reach the computer systems

275
00:10:00,297 --> 00:10:02,237
they used for cardiology,

276
00:10:02,237 --> 00:10:03,524
nuclear medicine,

277
00:10:03,524 --> 00:10:05,868
radiology and surgery.

278
00:10:06,117 --> 00:10:08,147
Somebody had launched it.

279
00:10:08,147 --> 00:10:10,151
And somebody would have to pay for

280
00:10:10,151 --> 00:10:10,952
it.

281
00:10:16,617 --> 00:10:19,273
Who is responsible for a weapon that

282
00:10:19,273 --> 00:10:21,805
does not stay where it was aimed?

283
00:10:25,617 --> 00:10:27,478
In February twenty eighteen,

284
00:10:27,478 --> 00:10:29,987
Britain's National Cyber Security Centre

285
00:10:29,987 --> 00:10:32,495
concluded that the Russian military was

286
00:10:32,495 --> 00:10:34,977
almost certainly responsible.

287
00:10:35,397 --> 00:10:37,167
The White House called it the most

288
00:10:37,167 --> 00:10:39,601
destructive and costly cyber attack in

289
00:10:39,601 --> 00:10:40,464
history,

290
00:10:40,464 --> 00:10:42,013
and said it was part of the

291
